EXCHANGING LIBRARY DISKS

CALLER. Subject to current caller access policy.

A - Exchanging library disks

:A
EXCHANGING LIBRARY DISKS

You aren't restricted to the disks which were mounted when you called. The
sysop may have made additional volumes available, ranging from small
collections of games to much larger archives.

    MOUNT /L

This lists the J: and P: catalogs and identifies their current images. To
select a volume, enter its catalog name:

    MOUNT GAMES

MOUNT chooses the appropriate drive for the image. The following forms are
useful if you wish to look at just one catalog:

    MOUNT J: /L
    MOUNT P: /L

If both catalogs have a volume of the same name, specify the drive as well,
for example MOUNT J: GAMES. The names in your board's catalog may be different
from the examples in this manual.

After a successful mount the program reports which drive received the new
volume. Try DIR J: for a J: disk and read its README.TXT if one is supplied.

To restore the original disks:

    MOUNT J: /DEFAULT
    MOUNT P: /DEFAULT

The second command restores the SIG/M and CPMUG archive on a standard
installation. The two drives can be exchanged independently. Logging off does
not necessarily restore them; a previous caller may have left a different
volume mounted.

MOUNT automatically runs NDSCAN to rebuild the selected drive's names. If the
scan reports an error after the mount has succeeded, don't assume that the old
names refer to the new disk. Use explicit DUs until the names have been
corrected or the default volume restored. You can run NDSCAN J: or NDSCAN P:
yourself on a fully readable drive.
